# 6. PHASE FOUR: DATABASE AND PERSISTENCE AUDIT

Inspect database connection handling, models, indexes, migrations, and transaction boundaries.

The previous audit found an in-memory Express order store and review store.

Verify whether these remain in any active production path.

## DB-001: Durable persistence

Production orders, payments, reviews, and other critical business records must be stored durably.

Identify any in-memory stores being used as the authoritative source of business data.

If such stores are active in production:

* Replace them with the established persistent storage layer.
* Preserve existing data.
* Avoid introducing a second source of truth.
* Handle database failures explicitly.
* Do not return false success responses when writes fail.

Do not migrate data destructively or invent records.

## DB-002: Startup readiness

Check whether the application can start and accept requests when MongoDB is unavailable or required indexes have failed to initialize.

Distinguish liveness from readiness.

A process being alive does not mean the application is ready to process orders.

Validate required production dependencies before accepting business traffic.

## DB-003: Indexes and duplicate data

Inspect all unique and non-unique indexes.

Check whether index initialization is hidden behind broad exception handling.

Detect existing duplicates before proposing unique indexes.

Do not automatically delete duplicate business records.

Produce a safe remediation plan if duplicates prevent an index from being created.

## DB-004: Concurrency

Check concurrent order creation, stock updates, coupon usage, and payment callbacks wherever those operations are supported.

Use transactions, conditional updates, unique constraints, or idempotency controls where appropriate.

Do not introduce an expensive transaction architecture without first checking the existing database capabilities and application design.

## DB-005: Rate limiting

Previously identified FastAPI rate limiting used a process-local dictionary.

Determine whether this is still active and whether it is sufficient for the deployed architecture.

If the service runs across multiple instances, recommend a shared rate-limit store where needed.

Ensure rate-limit storage failure does not silently create an unsafe production state.

